What changed: the previous version passed `AsyncOptions` on every enqueue, including the chained child jobs in `execute`. Salesforce doesn't allow the maximum stack depth to be set from inside a queueable, which caused "Cannot reset Maximum Queueable Stack Depth in a queueable". Now only `start()` sets `MaximumQueueableStackDepth = 5`, on the first job of each chain. Chained children call plain `System.enqueueJob` and inherit that depth.

I haven't run this against an org, so I can't confirm the chains finish inside `Test.stopTest()`. The depth of 5 and the 50-chain fan-out cover 50,000 Ids on paper (50 chains × 5 jobs × 200 Ids).

The compile error was in the describe call. `Schema.SObjectType.Account.fields.FB_Open_Pipeline__c` is already a `DescribeFieldResult`, so the extra `.getDescribe()` doesn't exist. I removed it, and the trigger error went away with it, since it only appeared because the class didn't compile. I haven't re-run the deploy or tests after this change.

The count field is now also set to its own scale, so the "only update if changed" comparison is consistent for both fields. That comparison is what stops the two triggers from re-triggering each other. `AccountTrigger` updates open opportunities on every account update, but that update changes no amount, stage or account, so nothing is recalculated.

